0|0@news^1438463674#cto8pql:veive$:&gt; Never mind the millions of cows, pigs, chickens and so on that get slaughtered needlessly all the time. false. [1](http://blog.nature.org/science/2013/08/22/too-many-deer/) [2](http://www.na.fs.fed.us/fhp/special_interests/white_tailed_deer.pdf) [3](http://www.science20.com/news_articles/if_you_want_to_save_the_forests_hunt_deer-140271) [4](http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/21139125) [5](http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/16441942) [6](http://ajcn.nutrition.org/content/89/5/1627S.full) [7](http://www.nytimes.com/2007/05/21/opinion/21planck.html?_r=0) Controlling the population of large herbivores is essential to the ecosystem. *Something* has to kill herbivores. It's scientifically proven fact. Furthermore humans are omnivores, people who have tried to raise infants on strictly vegan diets have been tried and convicted of the murder of those infants. The problem with killing a lion is that lions are an endangered species. If you kill too many lions, then there aren't enough lions to reproduce and the lions die out, which reduces the [biodiversity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biodiversity) of the ecosystem. Biodiversity is important for a few reasons. [8](http://www.globalissues.org/article/170/why-is-biodiversity-important-who-cares) [9](http://www.nwf.org/Wildlife/Wildlife-Conservation/Biodiversity.aspx) In contrast, we aren't at risk of killing too many cows or deer right now. [citation needed] It's also worth noting that in some cases it can actually be beneficial to cull specific individuals within an endangered species.[10](http://www.cnn.com/2014/01/10/us/black-rhino-hunting-permit/) tl;dr: you're wrong. You're viewing the world in terms of black and white. Modern issues are complex and require the ability to distinguish shades of grey.
